{"level":30,"time":1776957913209,"pid":43999,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":5,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017W3gNESSo9bqUFpEdrkZ4d","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Transformer Pad Clearances and Location Requirements\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers spatial and location requirements for transformer pad installations, including horizontal clearances from buildings and site features, vertical clearances for equipment access and overhead obstructions, separation from underground utilities, property location restrictions and prohibited areas, and transformer pad sizing and protection standards.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→Transformer pad clearances are governed by a multi-layered framework in the Utilities Criteria Manual (UCM) that addresses horizontal clearances from structures and site features, vertical clearances for crane access and overhead obstructions, separation from underground utilities, and property/easement location restrictions. The primary regulatory structure is UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4, which establishes clearance requirements based on equipment orientation, building materials, and spatial configuration.\n    10→\n    11→**Pad-Mounted Transformers:** Electrical transformers installed at ground level on concrete foundation pads (as distinguished from pole-mounted or vault transformers). They contain pad-locked access doors and are supplied in both single-phase and three-phase configurations. Specific pad dimensions are determined by Austin Energy Design on a project-specific basis, not standardized in code.\n    12→\n    13→**Three-Phase vs. Single-Phase Service:** Service type is determined by Austin Energy Design based on customer electrical load requirements and infrastructure availability, not from civil site plans alone. Three-phase service is furnished only where the customer's load and equipment warrant it and where AE has necessary facilities installed and available (UCM § 1.3.9).\n    14→\n    15→**Key Thresholds:**\n    16→\n    17→- **Hot-stick use area (access door side):** 10 feet to open area or adjacent building/structure; **15 feet when facing confined space** (niche, vault, or enclosed area on 3+ sides)\n    18→- **Sides with operators or controls:** 5 feet from removable ventilated obstruction (fence)\n    19→- **Sides without operators or controls:** 3 feet from removable ventilated obstruction (fence)\n    20→- **Fire escape clearance:** 20 feet horizontal/radial from edge of oil-filled equipment pad to fire escape or stairs serving as fire escape\n    21→- **Brick or masonry buildings:** 5 feet minimum horizontal clearance from side edges of equipment pad\n    22→- **Windows/doors/ventilating ducts:** 5 feet minimum lateral clearance (brick/masonry); 12 feet horizontal from edge of oil-filled equipment pad if window/door is less than 12 feet from grade or has less than 5 feet lateral separation\n    23→- **Non-brick/masonry structures:** 12 feet horizontal clearance from edge of oil-filled equipment pad\n    24→- **Niche vertical clearance:** Minimum 35 feet vertical clearance inside niche and niche entrance (not 20 or 30 feet)\n    25→- **Overhead structures:** NO covering, building, or structure allowed directly above pad or minimum equipment area\n    26→- **Sidewalks:** 2 feet from back of sidewalks\n    27→- **Overhead distribution lines:** Minimum 7.5 feet horizontal sky-to-ground clearance; **15-foot radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors\n    28→- **Underground utilities:** 12 inches minimum (horizontal and vertical) from other utilities; 24-36 inches from gas lines; 5 feet from Austin Water infrastructure\n    29→- **Trees:** 10 lateral feet from any side of pad-mounted equipment; shrubs 10 lateral feet from access door side, 5 lateral feet from other sides\n    30→- **Root barriers:** Required for trees within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment (4 feet deep, 5 feet from equipment)\n    31→- **Bollards:** 4-inch minimum diameter galvanized rigid metal posts required when equipment pad installed **within 4 feet** of parking/traffic areas (not \"3 feet in front\")\n    32→\n    33→**Exceptions:**\n    34→\n    35→- **3-foot clearance reduction:** 5-foot clearance to brick/masonry buildings may be reduced to 3 feet (for one side only) with Austin Energy Design written approval when: (1) not in confined space, (2) 10 ft+ to windows/doors/ducts, (3) 15 ft+ vertical clearance to windows/doors/ducts, and (4) minimum 3-hour fire rating in clearance area (UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 2)\n    36→- **Niche service:** Specialized configuration where AE equipment is accessible from outside but located within customer's building footprint. Permitted only outside the downtown Network Area. Requires 35-foot vertical clearance, 3-hour fire walls, proper ventilation, and consultation with AE Design business unit (UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 3)\n    37→- **Residential service line separations:** Minimum 3-foot horizontal separation may be allowed between water, reclaimed water, and wastewater service lines and dry utility service lines on residential projects (UCM § 2.9.1.B.2)\n    38→\n    39→**Property and Easement Requirements:**\n    40→\n    41→- All padmount/vault/niche equipment must be installed on customer's property, not in public right-of-way (UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 4)\n    42→- Electrical routing from transformer to meter must be 100% on private property\n    43→- Transformer easements required around pads (width determined by AE Design for accessibility)\n    44→- Equipment cannot be placed in drainage easements, water easements, sewer easements, or electrical easements under overhead lines without AE Design written approval\n    45→\n    46→**Calculation Methods:**\n    47→\n    48→- **Horizontal clearances:** Measured from outside edges of equipment pad to adjacent features (buildings, structures, site amenities)\n    49→- **Lateral clearances:** Measured horizontally from specific building features (windows, doors, ventilating ducts)\n    50→- **Radial clearances:** Measured from center point or edge of equipment in all directions (fire escapes, overhead conductors)\n    51→- **Vertical clearances:** Measured from grade level upward (windows requiring 12 feet when lateral clearance < 5 feet), or from underside of overhead structures downward (35 feet for niches, crane access)\n    52→- **Underground utility separations:** Measured outside diameter to outside diameter (OD to OD) of pipes/conduits\n    53→\n    54→**Common Violation Patterns:**\n    55→\n    56→- **3-foot fence clearance shown without AE Design written approval:** Plans show 3-foot clearance to building without documenting: (1) written AE Design approval, (2) verification of 3-hour fire rating, (3) confirmation not in confined space, and (4) adequate vertical clearances to windows/doors\n    57→- **Niche/enclosed areas without 35-foot clearance documentation:** Plans show transformer in niche, enclosed area, or notched-out building configuration without cross-section drawings demonstrating 35-foot vertical clearance from grade through entire access path\n    58→- **Transformer pad dimensions not matching AE Design details:** Plans show pad dimensions (e.g., \"5'x5'\" or \"10'x10'\") that do not match AE Design-approved transformer pad detail sheets for the specified service type and load\n    59→- **Bollards missing when pad within 4 feet of parking:** Plans show transformer pad located within 4 feet of parking aisles, drive lanes, or vehicle circulation areas without 4-inch diameter bollards per AE Design equipment pad details\n    60→- **Drainage sloping toward building from oil-filled equipment:** Grading plan shows drainage slopes or surface flow directed toward building from transformer pad location (code requires liquid flow away from building for oil-filled equipment per UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 1)\n    61→- **15-foot radius violation for overhead conductors:** Plans show structures, landscaping, or site features within 15-foot radius of overhead distribution primary/neutral conductors (measured from conductors, not pole centerline)\n    62→- **Trees within 10 feet not identified as utility-compatible:** Landscape plan shows trees within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment or within 15 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors without species confirmation from ECM Appendix F \"UC\" (Utility Compatible) column\n    63→- **Fire rating not documented for reduced clearances:** Plans show clearances less than 5 feet to buildings without architectural specifications confirming minimum 3-hour fire rating in the clearance area\n    64→\n    65→**Confined Space / Niche Identification:**\n    66→\n    67→A transformer location qualifies as \"facing a confined space\" (triggering 15-foot clearance instead of 10-foot) when the access door side faces an area enclosed on 3 or more sides with limited ventilation or restricted egress. Niches are specialized installations where AE equipment is accessible from outside but located within the customer's building footprint, requiring 35-foot vertical clearance, 3-hour fire walls, and AE Design consultation.\n    68→\n    69→**Oil-Filled vs. Dry-Type Equipment:**\n    70→\n    71→Certain Table 1.10.4 clearances specify increased requirements for \"oil-filled equipment\" due to fire risk from insulating oil/liquid. Oil-filled transformer types should be identified on electrical specifications or transformer callouts. Fire escape clearances (20 feet) and grading/drainage direction requirements (liquid flow away from building) apply specifically to oil-filled equipment.\n    72→\n    73→## Code References\n    74→\n    75→- UCM § 1.10.4 - Clearances From AE Padmount Equipment and Distribution Vaults (primary governing section)\n    76→- UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 - Minimum Clearances from Padmount Equipment Pads and Distribution Vaults\n    77→- UCM § 1.10.5 - Clearances for AE Underground Cable, Conduit, and Underground Facilities\n    78→- UCM § 1.10.3 - Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines and Facilities\n    79→- UCM § 1.10.10.4 - Trees in Proximity to Electric Utility Infrastructure\n    80→- UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 - Horizontal and Vertical Separation Distance (Austin Water infrastructure)\n    81→- UCM § 1.3.9 - Single-Phase/Three-Phase Service\n    82→- UCM § 1.5.2.6 - Pad-Mounted Transformer Service\n    83→- UCM § 1.18.0 - Appendix and Exhibits\n    84→- ECM Appendix F - Descriptive Categories of Tree Species (utility-compatible tree identification)\n    85→\n    86→## Documents to Review\n    87→\n    88→- **Site Plan Sheets** - Transformer pad locations, dimensions, clearances to buildings and site features, property lines\n    89→- **Electrical Plan Sheets** - Transformer locations, electrical routing to meters, transformer easements, service type specifications\n    90→- **Utility Plan Sheets** - Underground utility locations, conflicts with transformer pads, utility crossings, overhead line locations\n    91→- **Landscape Plan Sheets** - Tree species identification, vegetation proximity to transformer pads, root barrier details\n    92→- **Detail Sheets** - Transformer pad construction details matching AE Design-approved specifications, bollard details, protective barrier specifications\n    93→- **Easement Exhibits** - Transformer easements, drainage easements, utility easements, prohibited areas, property boundaries\n    94→- **Building Elevation Sheets** - Vertical clearances from windows, balconies, overhangs, fire escape locations\n    95→- **Cross-Section Drawings** - Vertical clearances from overhead structures, parking garages, enclosed areas, niche configurations showing 35-foot clearance\n    96→- **Grading and Drainage Plans** - Surface drainage direction around transformer pads (for oil-filled equipment)\n    97→- **Architectural Specifications** - Fire ratings for walls/structures in reduced clearance areas (3-hour rating verification)\n    98→- **Austin Energy Design Approval Documents** - ESPA (Electric Service Planning Application), transformer pad detail sheets, service type approval, written approvals for clearance reductions or variances\n    99→\n   100→## Checklist Items\n   101→\n   102→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n   103→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n   104→| EL-13.1 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from buildings, structures, and building foundations | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4; UCM § 1.10.5.B | All sites |\n   105→| EL-13.2 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from retaining walls | Reviewer Convention | Only when retaining walls are present. Check: site plan and grading plan. |\n   106→| EL-13.3 | Transformer pads lack required clearance from fences and gates - 5 feet for sides with operators/controls, 3 feet for sides without operators/controls (from removable ventilated fences) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when fences or gates are adjacent to transformer pads. Check: site plan showing fence locations and transformer access door orientation. |\n   107→| EL-13.4 | Transformer pads with reduced clearances (3 feet to brick/masonry buildings) lack documentation of Austin Energy Design written approval or do not meet required conditions (not in confined space, 10 ft+ to windows/doors/ducts, 15 ft+ vertical clearance to windows/doors/ducts, 3-hour fire rating) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 2 | Only when clearances less than 5 feet to buildings are shown. Check: site plan dimensions and architectural specifications for fire rating. |\n   108→| EL-13.5 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot lateral clearance from balconies and building overhangs, or have coverings/structures directly above pad or minimum equipment area | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when balconies or overhangs are present above or adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan, building elevations, and building cross-sections. |\n   109→| EL-13.6 | Transformer pads lack minimum 12-foot horizontal clearance from windows, doors, or ventilating ducts when window/door/duct is less than 12 feet from grade or has less than 5 feet lateral separation (oil-filled equipment) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when windows, doors, or ventilating ducts are present on adjacent buildings with inadequate lateral separation or low grade elevation. Check: site plan, building elevations, and transformer equipment specifications. |\n   110→| EL-13.7 | Transformer pads not located minimum 2 feet from back of sidewalks | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when sidewalks are adjacent to transformer pads. Check: site plan. |\n   111→| EL-13.8 | Transformer pads lack required clearance from stairwells serving as fire exits | Reviewer Convention | Only when stairwells are present adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan and building floor plans. |\n   112→| EL-13.9 | Transformer pads lack minimum 20-foot horizontal/radial clearance from fire escapes or stairs serving as fire escapes (oil-filled equipment) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when fire escapes or stairs serving as fire escapes are present. Check: site plan and building elevations showing fire escape locations. |\n   113→| EL-13.10 | Transformer pad hot-stick use area (access door side) lacks minimum 10-foot clearance to open area or adjacent building/structure | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | All sites |\n   114→| EL-13.11 | Transformer pad hot-stick use area (access door side) lacks minimum 15-foot clearance when facing confined space (niche, vault, or enclosed area on 3+ sides) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when transformer location faces confined space such as niche, vault, or notched-out building area. Check: site plan configuration and building cross-sections. |\n   115→| EL-13.12 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from fire hydrants | Reviewer Convention | Only when fire hydrants are present. Check: site plan showing fire hydrant locations. |\n   116→| EL-13.13 | Trees within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment are not identified as utility-compatible species per ECM Appendix F, or trees within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductors are not utility-compatible | UCM § 1.10.10.4; ECM Appendix F | Only when trees are proposed within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment or 15 lateral feet of overhead distribution. Check: landscape plan species list cross-referenced with ECM Appendix F \"UC\" column. |\n   117→| EL-13.14 | Shrubs or low-lying vegetation lack minimum 10 lateral feet clearance from transformer access door side, or lack minimum 5 lateral feet clearance from other sides (non-access door) | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when shrubs, vegetation, or landscaping is adjacent to transformer. Check: landscape plan showing planting locations relative to transformer access door orientation. |\n   118→| EL-13.15 | Trees within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment lack required root barriers (minimum 4 feet deep, installed 5 feet from equipment) | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when trees are proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment. Check: landscape plan and detail sheets for root barrier specifications. |\n   119→| EL-13.16 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from dumpsters | Reviewer Convention | Only when dumpsters are present. Check: site plan showing dumpster locations and enclosures. |\n   120→| EL-13.17 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from bike racks | Reviewer Convention | Only when bike racks are present. Check: site plan. |\n   121→| EL-13.18 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from light poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when light poles are adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan and photometric plan. |\n   122→| EL-13.19 | Transformer pads lack minimum 15-foot clearance from fire lanes | Reviewer Convention | Only when fire lanes are designated. Check: site plan showing fire lane striping, signage, and plan notes. |\n   123→| EL-13.20 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from other transformer pads | Reviewer Convention | Only when multiple transformer pads are proposed. Check: electrical plan. |\n   124→| EL-13.21 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from water lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n   125→| EL-13.22 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from wastewater lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n   126→| EL-13.23 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from storm drain lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n   127→| EL-13.24 | Transformer pads lack minimum horizontal clearance from gas lines (24 inches minimum for gas pipelines less than 60 psi, 36 inches minimum for high-pressure gas 60 psi and over) | UCM § 1.10.5.A | Only when gas service is present. Check: utility plan showing gas infrastructure and pressure specifications. |\n   128→| EL-13.25 | Transformer pads lack minimum 12-inch horizontal and vertical clearance from other underground utilities (except gas, fuel, steam) | UCM § 1.10.5.A | All sites |\n   129→| EL-13.26 | Transformer pads or AE underground facilities located over or under other utility lines, or customer buildings/foundations/structures installed over AE underground facilities | UCM § 1.10.5.B | All sites |\n   130→| EL-13.27 | Transformer locations lack minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from overhead distribution line conductors (primary, neutral, and secondary), extending from ground to sky | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution lines are present. Check: site plan and utility plan showing overhead lines and proposed structures/features. |\n   131→| EL-13.28 | Transformer locations or proposed facilities lack minimum 15-foot radius clearance from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (measured from conductors, not pole centerline) | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present. Check: utility plan showing overhead line locations and proposed site features. |\n   132→| EL-13.29 | Transformer locations in enclosed or notched-out areas (niches) lack minimum 35-foot vertical clearance inside niche and niche entrance, or lack cross-section drawings demonstrating clearance | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 3 | Only when transformer is located in niche (accessible from outside but within building footprint), enclosed area, or notched-out building configuration. Check: site plan configuration, building cross-sections, and niche detail drawings. |\n   133→| EL-13.30 | Niche service installations lack required 3-hour fire wall rating for walls and ceilings, proper ventilation specifications, or documentation of consultation with Austin Energy Design business unit | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 3 | Only when niche service is proposed (equipment within building footprint, accessible from outside). Check: architectural specifications and electrical plan notes for AE Design coordination. |\n   134→| EL-13.31 | Cross-section drawings not provided showing vertical clearances in access paths with overhead structures, parking garages, or enclosed areas | UCM § 1.10.3 (survey requirement as required by AE Design) | Only when overhead structures, parking garages, enclosed areas, or niches are present above or adjacent to transformer locations. Check: plan set for cross-section sheets demonstrating vertical clearance compliance. |\n   135→| EL-13.32 | Grading plan shows drainage slopes toward building from oil-filled transformer pad location (liquid flow must be away from building) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 1 | Only when oil-filled transformer equipment is proposed. Check: grading and drainage plans showing surface flow direction around transformer pad, and transformer equipment specifications. |\n   136→| EL-13.33 | Transformer pads not located 100% on private property, located in public right-of-way, or straddling property lines | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 | All sites |\n   137→| EL-13.34 | Transformer pads located in prohibited easements including drainage easements, water easements, sewer easements, or electrical easements under overhead lines without Austin Energy Design written approval | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 (property requirement); easement-specific regulations | All sites |\n   138→| EL-13.35 | Transformer easements not shown around pads, or easement width inadequate for Austin Energy accessibility requirements | UCM § 1.10.3 (additional clearance and access easements may be required) | All sites |\n   139→| EL-13.36 | Electrical routing from transformer to meter not 100% on private property | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 | All sites |\n   140→| EL-13.37 | Transformer pad dimensions do not match Austin Energy Design-approved transformer pad detail sheets for specified service type (single-phase or three-phase) | UCM § 1.5.2.6; UCM § 1.18.0 (obtain transformer pad detail from AE Design) | All sites |\n   141→| EL-13.38 | Equipment pad installed within 4 feet of parking areas or vehicle routes lacks required 4-inch minimum diameter galvanized rigid metal posts (bollards) per AE Design equipment pad details, or bollards not spaced to allow equipment door opening | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when transformer pad is located within 4 feet of parking areas, drive aisles, or vehicle circulation routes. Check: site plan showing parking layout, circulation, and bollard locations/specifications. |\n   142→| EL-13.39 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks) installed under or over Austin Energy overhead distribution facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution facilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan showing overhead line locations and proposed structures. |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01b15f0e-4ea6-438f-b485-676df5e8b514","uuid":"d06815c6-fb73-4bee-86c7-1fc7060c8c98","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/13.md","content":"# Transformer Pad Clearances and Location Requirements\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers spatial and location requirements for transformer pad installations, including horizontal clearances from buildings and site features, vertical clearances for equipment access and overhead obstructions, separation from underground utilities, property location restrictions and prohibited areas, and transformer pad sizing and protection standards.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\nTransformer pad clearances are governed by a multi-layered framework in the Utilities Criteria Manual (UCM) that addresses horizontal clearances from structures and site features, vertical clearances for crane access and overhead obstructions, separation from underground utilities, and property/easement location restrictions. The primary regulatory structure is UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4, which establishes clearance requirements based on equipment orientation, building materials, and spatial configuration.\n\n**Pad-Mounted Transformers:** Electrical transformers installed at ground level on concrete foundation pads (as distinguished from pole-mounted or vault transformers). They contain pad-locked access doors and are supplied in both single-phase and three-phase configurations. Specific pad dimensions are determined by Austin Energy Design on a project-specific basis, not standardized in code.\n\n**Three-Phase vs. Single-Phase Service:** Service type is determined by Austin Energy Design based on customer electrical load requirements and infrastructure availability, not from civil site plans alone. Three-phase service is furnished only where the customer's load and equipment warrant it and where AE has necessary facilities installed and available (UCM § 1.3.9).\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n\n- **Hot-stick use area (access door side):** 10 feet to open area or adjacent building/structure; **15 feet when facing confined space** (niche, vault, or enclosed area on 3+ sides)\n- **Sides with operators or controls:** 5 feet from removable ventilated obstruction (fence)\n- **Sides without operators or controls:** 3 feet from removable ventilated obstruction (fence)\n- **Fire escape clearance:** 20 feet horizontal/radial from edge of oil-filled equipment pad to fire escape or stairs serving as fire escape\n- **Brick or masonry buildings:** 5 feet minimum horizontal clearance from side edges of equipment pad\n- **Windows/doors/ventilating ducts:** 5 feet minimum lateral clearance (brick/masonry); 12 feet horizontal from edge of oil-filled equipment pad if window/door is less than 12 feet from grade or has less than 5 feet lateral separation\n- **Non-brick/masonry structures:** 12 feet horizontal clearance from edge of oil-filled equipment pad\n- **Niche vertical clearance:** Minimum 35 feet vertical clearance inside niche and niche entrance (not 20 or 30 feet)\n- **Overhead structures:** NO covering, building, or structure allowed directly above pad or minimum equipment area\n- **Sidewalks:** 2 feet from back of sidewalks\n- **Overhead distribution lines:** Minimum 7.5 feet horizontal sky-to-ground clearance; **15-foot radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors\n- **Underground utilities:** 12 inches minimum (horizontal and vertical) from other utilities; 24-36 inches from gas lines; 5 feet from Austin Water infrastructure\n- **Trees:** 10 lateral feet from any side of pad-mounted equipment; shrubs 10 lateral feet from access door side, 5 lateral feet from other sides\n- **Root barriers:** Required for trees within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment (4 feet deep, 5 feet from equipment)\n- **Bollards:** 4-inch minimum diameter galvanized rigid metal posts required when equipment pad installed **within 4 feet** of parking/traffic areas (not \"3 feet in front\")\n\n**Exceptions:**\n\n- **3-foot clearance reduction:** 5-foot clearance to brick/masonry buildings may be reduced to 3 feet (for one side only) with Austin Energy Design written approval when: (1) not in confined space, (2) 10 ft+ to windows/doors/ducts, (3) 15 ft+ vertical clearance to windows/doors/ducts, and (4) minimum 3-hour fire rating in clearance area (UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 2)\n- **Niche service:** Specialized configuration where AE equipment is accessible from outside but located within customer's building footprint. Permitted only outside the downtown Network Area. Requires 35-foot vertical clearance, 3-hour fire walls, proper ventilation, and consultation with AE Design business unit (UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 3)\n- **Residential service line separations:** Minimum 3-foot horizontal separation may be allowed between water, reclaimed water, and wastewater service lines and dry utility service lines on residential projects (UCM § 2.9.1.B.2)\n\n**Property and Easement Requirements:**\n\n- All padmount/vault/niche equipment must be installed on customer's property, not in public right-of-way (UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 4)\n- Electrical routing from transformer to meter must be 100% on private property\n- Transformer easements required around pads (width determined by AE Design for accessibility)\n- Equipment cannot be placed in drainage easements, water easements, sewer easements, or electrical easements under overhead lines without AE Design written approval\n\n**Calculation Methods:**\n\n- **Horizontal clearances:** Measured from outside edges of equipment pad to adjacent features (buildings, structures, site amenities)\n- **Lateral clearances:** Measured horizontally from specific building features (windows, doors, ventilating ducts)\n- **Radial clearances:** Measured from center point or edge of equipment in all directions (fire escapes, overhead conductors)\n- **Vertical clearances:** Measured from grade level upward (windows requiring 12 feet when lateral clearance < 5 feet), or from underside of overhead structures downward (35 feet for niches, crane access)\n- **Underground utility separations:** Measured outside diameter to outside diameter (OD to OD) of pipes/conduits\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n\n- **3-foot fence clearance shown without AE Design written approval:** Plans show 3-foot clearance to building without documenting: (1) written AE Design approval, (2) verification of 3-hour fire rating, (3) confirmation not in confined space, and (4) adequate vertical clearances to windows/doors\n- **Niche/enclosed areas without 35-foot clearance documentation:** Plans show transformer in niche, enclosed area, or notched-out building configuration without cross-section drawings demonstrating 35-foot vertical clearance from grade through entire access path\n- **Transformer pad dimensions not matching AE Design details:** Plans show pad dimensions (e.g., \"5'x5'\" or \"10'x10'\") that do not match AE Design-approved transformer pad detail sheets for the specified service type and load\n- **Bollards missing when pad within 4 feet of parking:** Plans show transformer pad located within 4 feet of parking aisles, drive lanes, or vehicle circulation areas without 4-inch diameter bollards per AE Design equipment pad details\n- **Drainage sloping toward building from oil-filled equipment:** Grading plan shows drainage slopes or surface flow directed toward building from transformer pad location (code requires liquid flow away from building for oil-filled equipment per UCM § 1.10.4 footnote 1)\n- **15-foot radius violation for overhead conductors:** Plans show structures, landscaping, or site features within 15-foot radius of overhead distribution primary/neutral conductors (measured from conductors, not pole centerline)\n- **Trees within 10 feet not identified as utility-compatible:** Landscape plan shows trees within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment or within 15 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors without species confirmation from ECM Appendix F \"UC\" (Utility Compatible) column\n- **Fire rating not documented for reduced clearances:** Plans show clearances less than 5 feet to buildings without architectural specifications confirming minimum 3-hour fire rating in the clearance area\n\n**Confined Space / Niche Identification:**\n\nA transformer location qualifies as \"facing a confined space\" (triggering 15-foot clearance instead of 10-foot) when the access door side faces an area enclosed on 3 or more sides with limited ventilation or restricted egress. Niches are specialized installations where AE equipment is accessible from outside but located within the customer's building footprint, requiring 35-foot vertical clearance, 3-hour fire walls, and AE Design consultation.\n\n**Oil-Filled vs. Dry-Type Equipment:**\n\nCertain Table 1.10.4 clearances specify increased requirements for \"oil-filled equipment\" due to fire risk from insulating oil/liquid. Oil-filled transformer types should be identified on electrical specifications or transformer callouts. Fire escape clearances (20 feet) and grading/drainage direction requirements (liquid flow away from building) apply specifically to oil-filled equipment.\n\n## Code References\n\n- UCM § 1.10.4 - Clearances From AE Padmount Equipment and Distribution Vaults (primary governing section)\n- UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 - Minimum Clearances from Padmount Equipment Pads and Distribution Vaults\n- UCM § 1.10.5 - Clearances for AE Underground Cable, Conduit, and Underground Facilities\n- UCM § 1.10.3 - Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines and Facilities\n- UCM § 1.10.10.4 - Trees in Proximity to Electric Utility Infrastructure\n- UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 - Horizontal and Vertical Separation Distance (Austin Water infrastructure)\n- UCM § 1.3.9 - Single-Phase/Three-Phase Service\n- UCM § 1.5.2.6 - Pad-Mounted Transformer Service\n- UCM § 1.18.0 - Appendix and Exhibits\n- ECM Appendix F - Descriptive Categories of Tree Species (utility-compatible tree identification)\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Site Plan Sheets** - Transformer pad locations, dimensions, clearances to buildings and site features, property lines\n- **Electrical Plan Sheets** - Transformer locations, electrical routing to meters, transformer easements, service type specifications\n- **Utility Plan Sheets** - Underground utility locations, conflicts with transformer pads, utility crossings, overhead line locations\n- **Landscape Plan Sheets** - Tree species identification, vegetation proximity to transformer pads, root barrier details\n- **Detail Sheets** - Transformer pad construction details matching AE Design-approved specifications, bollard details, protective barrier specifications\n- **Easement Exhibits** - Transformer easements, drainage easements, utility easements, prohibited areas, property boundaries\n- **Building Elevation Sheets** - Vertical clearances from windows, balconies, overhangs, fire escape locations\n- **Cross-Section Drawings** - Vertical clearances from overhead structures, parking garages, enclosed areas, niche configurations showing 35-foot clearance\n- **Grading and Drainage Plans** - Surface drainage direction around transformer pads (for oil-filled equipment)\n- **Architectural Specifications** - Fire ratings for walls/structures in reduced clearance areas (3-hour rating verification)\n- **Austin Energy Design Approval Documents** - ESPA (Electric Service Planning Application), transformer pad detail sheets, service type approval, written approvals for clearance reductions or variances\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-13.1 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from buildings, structures, and building foundations | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4; UCM § 1.10.5.B | All sites |\n| EL-13.2 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from retaining walls | Reviewer Convention | Only when retaining walls are present. Check: site plan and grading plan. |\n| EL-13.3 | Transformer pads lack required clearance from fences and gates - 5 feet for sides with operators/controls, 3 feet for sides without operators/controls (from removable ventilated fences) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when fences or gates are adjacent to transformer pads. Check: site plan showing fence locations and transformer access door orientation. |\n| EL-13.4 | Transformer pads with reduced clearances (3 feet to brick/masonry buildings) lack documentation of Austin Energy Design written approval or do not meet required conditions (not in confined space, 10 ft+ to windows/doors/ducts, 15 ft+ vertical clearance to windows/doors/ducts, 3-hour fire rating) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 2 | Only when clearances less than 5 feet to buildings are shown. Check: site plan dimensions and architectural specifications for fire rating. |\n| EL-13.5 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot lateral clearance from balconies and building overhangs, or have coverings/structures directly above pad or minimum equipment area | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when balconies or overhangs are present above or adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan, building elevations, and building cross-sections. |\n| EL-13.6 | Transformer pads lack minimum 12-foot horizontal clearance from windows, doors, or ventilating ducts when window/door/duct is less than 12 feet from grade or has less than 5 feet lateral separation (oil-filled equipment) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when windows, doors, or ventilating ducts are present on adjacent buildings with inadequate lateral separation or low grade elevation. Check: site plan, building elevations, and transformer equipment specifications. |\n| EL-13.7 | Transformer pads not located minimum 2 feet from back of sidewalks | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when sidewalks are adjacent to transformer pads. Check: site plan. |\n| EL-13.8 | Transformer pads lack required clearance from stairwells serving as fire exits | Reviewer Convention | Only when stairwells are present adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan and building floor plans. |\n| EL-13.9 | Transformer pads lack minimum 20-foot horizontal/radial clearance from fire escapes or stairs serving as fire escapes (oil-filled equipment) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when fire escapes or stairs serving as fire escapes are present. Check: site plan and building elevations showing fire escape locations. |\n| EL-13.10 | Transformer pad hot-stick use area (access door side) lacks minimum 10-foot clearance to open area or adjacent building/structure | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | All sites |\n| EL-13.11 | Transformer pad hot-stick use area (access door side) lacks minimum 15-foot clearance when facing confined space (niche, vault, or enclosed area on 3+ sides) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when transformer location faces confined space such as niche, vault, or notched-out building area. Check: site plan configuration and building cross-sections. |\n| EL-13.12 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from fire hydrants | Reviewer Convention | Only when fire hydrants are present. Check: site plan showing fire hydrant locations. |\n| EL-13.13 | Trees within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment are not identified as utility-compatible species per ECM Appendix F, or trees within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductors are not utility-compatible | UCM § 1.10.10.4; ECM Appendix F | Only when trees are proposed within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment or 15 lateral feet of overhead distribution. Check: landscape plan species list cross-referenced with ECM Appendix F \"UC\" column. |\n| EL-13.14 | Shrubs or low-lying vegetation lack minimum 10 lateral feet clearance from transformer access door side, or lack minimum 5 lateral feet clearance from other sides (non-access door) | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when shrubs, vegetation, or landscaping is adjacent to transformer. Check: landscape plan showing planting locations relative to transformer access door orientation. |\n| EL-13.15 | Trees within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment lack required root barriers (minimum 4 feet deep, installed 5 feet from equipment) | UCM § 1.10.10.4 | Only when trees are proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment. Check: landscape plan and detail sheets for root barrier specifications. |\n| EL-13.16 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from dumpsters | Reviewer Convention | Only when dumpsters are present. Check: site plan showing dumpster locations and enclosures. |\n| EL-13.17 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from bike racks | Reviewer Convention | Only when bike racks are present. Check: site plan. |\n| EL-13.18 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from light poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when light poles are adjacent to transformer. Check: site plan and photometric plan. |\n| EL-13.19 | Transformer pads lack minimum 15-foot clearance from fire lanes | Reviewer Convention | Only when fire lanes are designated. Check: site plan showing fire lane striping, signage, and plan notes. |\n| EL-13.20 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot clearance from other transformer pads | Reviewer Convention | Only when multiple transformer pads are proposed. Check: electrical plan. |\n| EL-13.21 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from water lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n| EL-13.22 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from wastewater lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n| EL-13.23 | Transformer pads lack minimum 5-foot horizontal clearance from storm drain lines | UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 | All sites |\n| EL-13.24 | Transformer pads lack minimum horizontal clearance from gas lines (24 inches minimum for gas pipelines less than 60 psi, 36 inches minimum for high-pressure gas 60 psi and over) | UCM § 1.10.5.A | Only when gas service is present. Check: utility plan showing gas infrastructure and pressure specifications. |\n| EL-13.25 | Transformer pads lack minimum 12-inch horizontal and vertical clearance from other underground utilities (except gas, fuel, steam) | UCM § 1.10.5.A | All sites |\n| EL-13.26 | Transformer pads or AE underground facilities located over or under other utility lines, or customer buildings/foundations/structures installed over AE underground facilities | UCM § 1.10.5.B | All sites |\n| EL-13.27 | Transformer locations lack minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from overhead distribution line conductors (primary, neutral, and secondary), extending from ground to sky | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution lines are present. Check: site plan and utility plan showing overhead lines and proposed structures/features. |\n| EL-13.28 | Transformer locations or proposed facilities lack minimum 15-foot radius clearance from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (measured from conductors, not pole centerline) | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present. Check: utility plan showing overhead line locations and proposed site features. |\n| EL-13.29 | Transformer locations in enclosed or notched-out areas (niches) lack minimum 35-foot vertical clearance inside niche and niche entrance, or lack cross-section drawings demonstrating clearance | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 3 | Only when transformer is located in niche (accessible from outside but within building footprint), enclosed area, or notched-out building configuration. Check: site plan configuration, building cross-sections, and niche detail drawings. |\n| EL-13.30 | Niche service installations lack required 3-hour fire wall rating for walls and ceilings, proper ventilation specifications, or documentation of consultation with Austin Energy Design business unit | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 3 | Only when niche service is proposed (equipment within building footprint, accessible from outside). Check: architectural specifications and electrical plan notes for AE Design coordination. |\n| EL-13.31 | Cross-section drawings not provided showing vertical clearances in access paths with overhead structures, parking garages, or enclosed areas | UCM § 1.10.3 (survey requirement as required by AE Design) | Only when overhead structures, parking garages, enclosed areas, or niches are present above or adjacent to transformer locations. Check: plan set for cross-section sheets demonstrating vertical clearance compliance. |\n| EL-13.32 | Grading plan shows drainage slopes toward building from oil-filled transformer pad location (liquid flow must be away from building) | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 1 | Only when oil-filled transformer equipment is proposed. Check: grading and drainage plans showing surface flow direction around transformer pad, and transformer equipment specifications. |\n| EL-13.33 | Transformer pads not located 100% on private property, located in public right-of-way, or straddling property lines | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 | All sites |\n| EL-13.34 | Transformer pads located in prohibited easements including drainage easements, water easements, sewer easements, or electrical easements under overhead lines without Austin Energy Design written approval | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 (property requirement); easement-specific regulations | All sites |\n| EL-13.35 | Transformer easements not shown around pads, or easement width inadequate for Austin Energy accessibility requirements | UCM § 1.10.3 (additional clearance and access easements may be required) | All sites |\n| EL-13.36 | Electrical routing from transformer to meter not 100% on private property | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 4 | All sites |\n| EL-13.37 | Transformer pad dimensions do not match Austin Energy Design-approved transformer pad detail sheets for specified service type (single-phase or three-phase) | UCM § 1.5.2.6; UCM § 1.18.0 (obtain transformer pad detail from AE Design) | All sites |\n| EL-13.38 | Equipment pad installed within 4 feet of parking areas or vehicle routes lacks required 4-inch minimum diameter galvanized rigid metal posts (bollards) per AE Design equipment pad details, or bollards not spaced to allow equipment door opening | UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 | Only when transformer pad is located within 4 feet of parking areas, drive aisles, or vehicle circulation routes. Check: site plan showing parking layout, circulation, and bollard locations/specifications. |\n| EL-13.39 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks) installed under or over Austin Energy overhead distribution facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution facilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan showing overhead line locations and proposed structures. |","numLines":142,"startLine":1,"totalLines":142}}}
