ility line (indicated by the **\"- OH -\"** line style) branches off and runs eastward.\n*   This line runs roughly parallel to the northern property boundary line (which is labeled with the bearing **\"S63°03'28\"E 419.86'\"**), passing through the tree canopy shown on the plan.\n*   This eastward-running overhead line terminates at (or passes through) another utility pole located near the northeast corner of the primary property area. This pole on the eastern side is also explicitly labeled with text reading **\"UTILITY POLE\"**.\n\nIn summary, the survey clearly indicates a main overhead utility line running the length of the Valley View Road frontage with at least three poles, and a secondary overhead line running along the northern property boundary connecting to an additional pole on the east side."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"86ce6d80-0b13-4282-a0c1-971fcaa319e8","uuid":"de624909-1623-4cac-be45-ae36a07fba57","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a careful visual analysis of the provided \"Existing Conditions Survey\" document, there is indeed existing overhead electric/utility infrastructure shown on the site plan.\n\nHere is a detailed description of what is shown, referencing the document's Legend and specific locations:\n\n**1. Identification via Legend:**\nIn the top-left area of the drawing, the \"LEGEND\" explicitly defines the symbols used for these features:\n*   A dashed line containing the text **\"- OH -\"** is defined as an **\"OVERHEAD UTILITY LINE\"**.\n*   A circle containing a cross/plus symbol is defined as a **\"UTILITY POLE\"**.\n\n**2. Infrastructure along Valley View Road (Western Boundary):**\nThere is a continuous overhead utility line running roughly north-south along the western edge of the property, parallel to and within or immediately adjacent to the right-of-way of **\"VALLEY VIEW ROAD\"**.\n*   Following this line from south to north along the road frontage, there are three distinct utility poles depicted:\n    *   There is a pole near the southwest corner of the mapped area, explicitly labeled with text pointing to it that reads **\"UTILITY POLE\"**.\n    *   Continuing north along the **\"- OH -\"** line, there is a second pole located roughly midway along the property's western boundary facing the road. This is also explicitly labeled **\"UTILITY POLE\"**.\n    *   Continuing further north, there is a third pole located near the northwest corner of the property boundary. This is also explicitly labeled **\"UTILITY POLE\"**.\n\n**3. Infrastructure along the Northern Boundary:**\n*   From the utility pole located near the northwest corner (mentioned above), an overhead utility line (indicated by the **\"- OH -\"** line style) branches off and runs eastward.\n*   This line runs roughly parallel to the northern property boundary line (which is labeled with the bearing **\"S63°03'28\"E 419.86'\"**), passing through the tree canopy shown on the plan.\n*   This eastward-running overhead line terminates at (or passes through) another utility pole located near the northeast corner of the primary property area. This pole on the eastern side is also explicitly labeled with text reading **\"UTILITY POLE\"**.\n\nIn summary, the survey clearly indicates a main overhead utility line running the length of the Valley View Road frontage with at least three poles, and a secondary overhead line running along the northern property boundary connecting to an additional pole on the east side."}]}
{"level":30,"time":1776957993331,"pid":43999,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01DwSMC4tZzC3X9EzZ6HMcPC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014jwxHFMJk7YmBm3FDkEjsz","name":"mcp__conductor_tools__run_measure_distance","input":{"reasoning":"The landscape plan shows overhead electric (OHE) infrastructure along the southern boundary of the site. Vision analysis indicated that mitigation trees (M) in the southeast corner appear very close to the OHE line. I need to verify whether these trees meet the minimum clearance requirements from the electric utility poles and conductors. For distribution lines, non-utility-compatible trees must be ≥15 feet from conductors, and any trees must be ≥10 feet from poles. If these are large trees (mature height ≥40 feet), they must be ≥25 feet from distribution conductors. Measuring actual distances will determine compliance or identify violations.","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":31,"scaleInchesPerFoot":0.05,"objectPairs":[{"objectA":"utility pole on OHE line in southeast portion of site near southern property boundary","objectB":"mitigation tree (M) symbol in southeast corner near OHE line"},{"objectA":"utility pole on OHE line along southern boundary","objectB":"mitigation tree (M) symbol in lower right area"},{"objectA":"OHE conductor line along southern boundary","objectB":"nearest mitigation or other tree in bottom-right area"}],"applicable_checklist_items":[{"checklist_id":"EL-2.1","checklist_deficiency_text":"Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment"},{"checklist_id":"EL-2.3","checklist_deficiency_text":"Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) proposed within 25 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or 