re is another service drop line labeled `— OHE —` branching westward from a utility pole located midway along the eastern boundary, connecting directly to the eastern side of the main building (\"SLAB\").\n\n### 2. Utility Poles with Overhead Lines\nNumerous utility poles are identified along the overhead wire routes, indicated by a circle/dot symbol and explicitly labeled \"UTILITY POLE\".\n*   **Along Valley View Road (West):**\n    *   One utility pole is located near the northwest corner, complete with a \"GUY\" wire anchor extending north.\n    *   A second utility pole is located midway down the western boundary, just south of the driveway entrance (this pole feeds the western service drop).\n    *   A third utility pole is located further south along the western boundary.\n    *   A fourth utility pole is located near the southwest corner.\n*   **Along Eastern Boundary:**\n    *   One utility pole is located near the southeast corner.\n    *   A second utility pole is located midway up the eastern boundary (this pole feeds the eastern service drop).\n    *   A third utility pole is located near the northeast corner.\n\n### 3. Light Poles with Overhead Service Drops\n*   There are symbols labeled \"LIGHT POLE\" on the property (e.g., one near the northern driveway entrance, another near the southern boundary).\n*   However, **there are no visible overhead lines (`— OHE —`) depicted connecting to these light poles** on this survey. They appear to either be fed underground or are attached directly to existing utility poles without separate overhead drops being drawn.\n\n### 4. Electric or Utility Easements\nThere are documented easements containing the overhead infrastructure:\n*   **Western Boundary:** There is a **`7.5' P.U.E.`** running parallel to Valley View Road along the western property edge. The western utility poles and overhead lines are located within or immediately adjacent to this easement.\n*   **Eastern Boundary:** There is a **`5' P.U.E. & ELEC. EASEMENT`** (as noted near the NE corner and mid-east boundary) running along the eastern property line. The eastern utility poles and overhead lines are located within this easement.\n\n### 5. Location and Extent relative to Property Boundaries\n*   The overhead electric facilities form a perimeter along the two longest sides of the lot (East and West boundaries).\n*   The western line extends the full length of the lot's frontage along Valley View Road, positioned just inside the property line within the 7.5' P.U.E.\n*   The eastern line extends almost the full length of the eastern rear/side property boundary, positioned within the 5' P.U.E.\n*   Overhead lines cross the interior of the property boundary from both the east and the west exclusively as service drops terminating at the central building slab."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c394a41f-2cb6-411b-ae31-3b8268d295eb","uuid":"4567985e-2b91-4b22-8a25-a0ff67144b9d","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided \"BOUNDARY, IMPROVEMENTS, TOPOGRAPHIC & TREE SURVEY\" for Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision (Valley View Townhomes), here is the identification of existing overhead electric facilities:\n\n### Legend vs. Drawing Documentation\n\n**Documented in Legend:**\nThe legend (located in the top left corner) documents several utility types. Those relevant to overhead electrical facilities include:\n*   `P.U.E.` - PUBLIC UTILITY EASEMENT\n*   `— OHE —` - OVERHEAD ELECTRIC LINE\n*   `— OHT —` - OVERHEAD TELECOM LINE\n*   `LP` (inside a circle) - LIGHT POLE\n*   `E` (inside a square) - ELECTRIC PEDESTAL\n*   `T` (inside a square) - TRANSFORMER OR PAD\n\n**Present on Drawing:**\nThe drawing actively uses the `— OHE —` line type, `P.U.E.` labels, explicit \"UTILITY POLE\" text labels with symbols (solid dots), and \"GUY\" wire annotations. The `— OHT —` (Overhead Telecom) line is also present, running parallel to the electric lines in some areas. Light poles are also depicted.\n\n### 1. Overhead Utility Lines (Power Lines)\nOverhead electric lines, explicitly labeled with **`— OHE —`**, are extensively mapped on the property.\n*   **Western Route:** A continuous overhead electric line (`— OHE —`) runs north-south along the entire western boundary of the property, parallel to \"VALLEY VIEW ROAD\".\n*   **Eastern Route:** Another continuous overhead electric line (`— OHE —`) runs north-south along the eastern boundary line.\n*   **Service Drops:**\n    *   There is a service drop line labeled `— OHE —` branching eastward from a utility pole on the western boundary (near the main driveway) connecting directly to the main building (\"SLAB\").\n    *   There is another service drop line labeled `— OHE —` branching westward from a utility pole located midway along the eastern boundary, connecting directly to the eastern side of the main building (\"SLAB\").\n\n### 2. Utility Poles with Overhead Lines\nNumerous utility poles are identified along the overhead wire routes, indicated by a circle/dot symbol and explicitly labeled \"UTILITY POLE\".\n*   **Along Valley View Road (West):**\n    *   One utility pole is located near the northwest corner, complete with a \"GUY\" wire anchor extending north.\n    *   A second utility pole is located midway down the western boundary, just south of the driveway entrance (this pole feeds the western service drop).\n    *   A third utility pole is located further south along the western boundary.\n    *   A fourth utility pole is located near the southwest corner.\n*   **Along Eastern Boundary:**\n    *   One utility pole is located near the southeast corner.\n    *   A second utility pole is located midway up the eastern boundary (this pole feeds the eastern service drop).\n    *   A third utility pole is located near the northeast corner.\n\n### 3. Light Poles with Overhead Service Drops\n*   There are symbols labeled \"LIGHT POLE\" on the property (e.g., one near the northern driveway entrance, another near the southern boundary).\n*   However, **there are no visible overhead lines (`— OHE —`) depicted connecting to these light poles** on this survey. They appear to either be fed underground or are attached directly to existing utility poles without separate overhead drops being drawn.\n\n### 4. Electric or Utility Easements\nThere are documented easements containing the overhead infrastructure:\n*   **Western Boundary:** There is a **`7.5' P.U.E.`** running parallel to Valley View Road along the western property edge. The western utility poles and overhead lines are located within or immediately adjacent to this easement.\n*   **Eastern Boundary:** There is a **`5' P.U.E. & ELEC. EASEMENT`** (as noted near the NE corner and mid-east boundary) running along the eastern property line. The eastern utility poles and overhead lines are located within this easement.\n\n### 5. Location and Extent relative to Property Boundaries\n*   The overhead electric facilities form a perimeter along the two longest sides of the lot (East and West boundaries).\n*   The western line extends the full length of the lot's frontage along Valley View Road, positioned just inside the property line within the 7.5' P.U.E.\n*   The eastern line extends almost the full length of the eastern rear/side property boundary, positioned within the 5' P.U.E.\n*   Overhead lines cross the interior of the property boundary from both the east and the west exclusively as service drops terminating at the central building slab."}]}
