{"level":30,"time":1776960763600,"pid":43999,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":35,"prompt":"Examine the plant list/schedule on this sheet. For each tree species listed (shade trees and ornamental trees), what information is provided about height? Specifically: 1) Does the plant list show \"mature height\" or \"mature height at maturity\"? 2) What height values are shown for each species? 3) Are these heights labeled as planting heights, minimum heights, or mature heights? 4) For Live Oak, Cedar Elm, Texas Mountain Laurel, and Mexican Redbud specifically, what height information is provided? This is needed to verify whether large trees (40+ feet at maturity per ECM Appendix F) are properly differentiated on the landscape plans.","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on the \"PLANT LIST\" table provided in the top-left corner of the document, here are the answers to your specific questions:\n\n**1) Does the plant list show \"mature height\" or \"mature height at maturity\"?**\nNo. There is no column or text in the plant list that indicates \"mature height\" or \"height at maturity\".\n\n**2 & 3) What height values are shown for each species and how are they labeled?**\nThe height values are located in the column labeled exactly as **\"MIN. SPEC.\"**. The height values themselves are explicitly labeled with the word **\"MINIMUM\"** (e.g., \"16' HT. MINIMUM\"), indicating they are planting specifications/minimum sizes required at the time of planting, rather than mature heights. \n\n**4) Specific height information provided for the requested trees:**\nHere is the exact text provided in the \"MIN. SPEC.\" column for each of the requested tree species:\n\n*   **LIVE OAK** (Listed under SHADE TREES):\n    \"100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN\"\n\n*   **CEDAR ELM** (Listed under SHADE TREES):\n    \"100 GAL.