mer 1**.\n2.  From **Transformer 1**, the E line routes horizontally to the west, crossing a Storm Drain (SD) line, and travels through the gap between Bldgs. 3 & 5 (north) and Bldgs. 4 & 6 (south) to connect to **Transformer 2**.\n3.  From **Transformer 2**, the E line continues west, crossing a Water (W) line and a Wastewater (WW) line. It then turns south, running between the western buildings (Bldgs. 1/2 and Bldgs. 7/8) and the central buildings (Bldgs. 3/4).\n4.  It continues southward, crosses an east-west Water (W) line, and finally connects to **Transformer 3** at the south end of the property.\n\n### 5. Fire Hydrant Locations and Distance to Transformers\nThere are two fire hydrants noted on the plan:\n\n1.  **Internal Proposed Fire Hydrant:** There is a new fire hydrant assembly located internally on the site, southwest of **Bldg. 7** and northwest of **Bldg. 8**. It is labeled with a note reading: *\"12.24 lf 8\" DI Fire Lead / Fire Hydrant Assembly / See Detail 511-AW-02\"*.\n    *   **Distance to Transformer 3 (South):** This is the closest transformer. Based on the 1\" = 20' scale, the hydrant is located approximately **60 to 80 feet** west/northwest of Transformer 3.\n    *   **Distance to Transformer 2 (Central):** Located diagonally to the northeast, approximately **150 to 200 feet** away.\n    *   **Distance to Transformer 1 (East):** Located on the far opposite side of the site, roughly **> 300 feet** away.\n\n2.  **External Existing Fire Hydrant:** There is a note on the far west edge of the property (near the existing street and water main tap) that reads: *\"Replace Existing Fire Hydrant with New Fire Hydrant\"*. This hydrant is strictly on the right-of-way boundary and is significantly far (several hundred feet) from all three internal transformer pads."}]}
