{"level":30,"time":1776961761493,"pid":43999,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XBsYPaXZJNFMAF7tj7hJnR","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 35 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 4: notes — Plant list section heading.\n     4→\n     5→PLANT LIST\n     6→\n     7→## Block 5: notes — Notes related to the plant list.\n     8→\n     9→NOTES:\n    10→1. THE CONTRACTORS (GENERAL AND SUBCONTRACTORS) SHALL PROVIDE UNIT COSTS FOR ALL\n    11→SOFTSCAPE AND HARDSCAPE MATERIAL SPECIFIED ON THE DRAWINGS AND SPECIFICATIONS. UNIT\n    12→COSTS SHALL BE PROVIDED FOR MATERIALS AND INSTALLATION SEPARATELY. UNIT COSTS SHALL\n    13→BE: 'EACH' FOR PLANT MATERIAL, 'SQUARE FOOT' OR 'SQUARE YARD' FOR PAVEMENTS, LINEAR FOOT\n    14→FOR WALL AND FENCE, 'EACH' FOR SITE FURNISHINGS AND SITE AMENITIES, 'CUBIC YARD' OR 'CUBIC\n    15→FOOT' FOR SOIL, MULCH AND OTHER BULK PRODUCTS AND 'EACH' OR 'LUMP SUM' FOR\n    16→MISCELLANEOUS ITEMS. THE UNIT COST SHALL BE FORMATTED TO HAVE COLUMNS FOR; ITEMS, UNIT,\n    17→UNIT COST, TOTAL ITEM COST.\n    18→2. PLANT QUANTITIES ARE PROVIDED FOR OWNER CONVENIENCE ONLY. CONTRACTOR IS\n    19→RESPONSIBLE FOR VERIFYING AND PROVIDING PLANT QUANTITIES SHOWN ON THE LANDSCAPE PLAN.\n    20→3. CONTRACTOR IS RESPONSIBLE FOR LOCATING AND SECURING TREE AND PLANT MATERIAL IN\n    21→ADVANCE. BECAUSE OF THE LONG LEAD TIME ON THIS INSTALLATION AND THE RECENT SHORTAGE\n    22→ON TREE CROP, CONTRACTOR SHALL RESERVE OR CONTRACT GROW TREES WITH A GROWER.\n    23→\n    24→## Block 6: notes — Notes on special construction techniques.\n    25→\n    26→SPECIAL CONSTRUCTION TECHNIQUES\n    27→ECM 3.5.4 (D)\n    28→1. PRIOR TO EXCAVATION WITHIN TREE DRIP LINES OR THE REMOVAL OF\n    29→TREES ADJACENT TO OTHER TREES THAT ARE TO REMAIN, MAKE A\n    30→CLEAN CUT BETWEEN THE DISTURBED AND UNDISTURBED ROOT ZONES\n    31→WITH A ROCK SAW OR SIMILAR EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n    32→2. IN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS THAT CANNOT BE PROTECTED DURING\n    33→CONSTRUCTION WITH FENCING AND WHERE HEAVY VEHICULAR TRAFFIC\n    34→IS ANTICIPATED, COVER THOSE AREAS WITH A MINIMUM OF 12 INCHES OF\n    35→ORGANIC MULCH TO MINIMIZE SOIL COMPACTION. IN AREAS WITH HIGH\n    36→SOIL PLASTICITY GEOTEXTILE FABRIC, PER STANDARD SPECIFICATION\n    37→620S, SHOULD BE PLACED UNDER THE MULCH TO PREVENT EXCESSIVE\n    38→MIXING OF THE SOIL AND MULCH. ADDITIONALLY, MATERIAL SUCH AS\n    39→PLYWOOD AND METAL SHEETS, COULD BE REQUIRED BY THE CITY\n    40→ARBORIST TO MINIMIZE ROOT IMPACTS FROM HEAVY EQUIPMENT.\n    41→3. ONCE THE PROJECT IS COMPLETED, ALL MATERIALS SHOULD BE\n    42→REMOVED, AND THE MULCH SHOULD BE REDUCED TO A DEPTH OF 3\n    43→INCHES. PERFORM ALL GRADING WITHIN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS BY\n    44→HAND OR WITH SMALL EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n    45→4. WATER ALL TREES MOST HEAVILY IMPACTED BY CONSTRUCTION\n    46→ACTIVITIES DEEPLY ONCE A WEEK DURING ACCUMULATION OF THE\n    47→LEAVES.\n    48→5. WHEN INSTALLING CONCRETE ADJACENT TO THE ROOT ZONE OF A TREE,\n    49→USE A PLASTIC VAPOR BARRIER BEHIND THE CONCRETE TO PROHIBIT\n    50→LEACHING OF LIME INTO THE SOIL.\n    51→\n    52→## Block 8: notes — Notes related to tree preservation calculations.\n    53→\n    54→NOTES:\n    55→1. CHINABERRY AND BRADFORD PEAR ARE INVASIVE TREES AND REQUIRE NO MITIGATION.\n    56→2. ASH TREES REQUIRE NO MITIGATION DUE TO EMERALD ASH BORER.\n    57→\n    58→## Block 11: table — Table listing shade trees.\n    59→\n    60→SHADE TREES\n    61→\n    62→| SYM. | QTY. | COMMON NAME, BOTANICAL NAME | MIN. SPEC. |\n    63→|---|---|---|---|\n    64→| [circle with dot] | | EXISTING TREE, TO BE PERSERVED | |\n    65→| [circle with cross] | 2 | LIVE OAK, QUERCUS VIRGINIANA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n    66→| [circle with hash] | 10 | CEDAR ELM, ULMUS CRASSIFOLIA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n    67→\n    68→## Block 12: table — Calculations related to landscaping.\n    69→\n    70→CITY OF AUSTIN\n    71→LANDSCAPE CALCULATIONS:\n    72→\n    73→STREET YARD AREA:\n    74→| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n    75→|---|---|---|\n    76→| TOTAL SITE AREA | N/A | 94,203 SF |\n    77→| TOTAL STREET-YARD AREA | N/A | 6,314 SF |\n    78→| STREET-YARD / LANDSCAPE (20%) | 1,263 SF | 5,206 SF |\n    79→\n    80→STREET YARD TREES:\n    81→EXISTING TREES CREDIT\n    82→| | | |\n    83→|---|---|---|\n    84→| 2\" DIAMETER TO 6\" DIAMETER | 0 | |\n    85→| 6\" DIAMETER OR GREATER | 4 | |\n    86→\n    87→EXISTING TREES CREDIT | PROVIDED |\n    88→|---|---|\n    89→| | 8 CREDITS |\n    90→\n    91→PROPOSED TREES CREDIT | PROVIDED |\n    92→|---|---|\n    93→| | 0 CREDITS |\n    94→\n    95→TOTAL STREET YARD TREES, EXISTING TREES CREDIT PLUS PROPOSED TREES CREDIT:\n    96→| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n    97→|---|---|---|\n    98→| | 5 | 8 |\n    99→\n   100→LANDSCAPE IN PARKING LOTS:\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8dbaef9-50fb-4056-bee6-2c0dccabc43f","uuid":"4b21f74c-3e20-497f-ae9d-0c0aa354a667","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-35/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 35 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 4: notes — Plant list section heading.\n\nPLANT LIST\n\n## Block 5: notes — Notes related to the plant list.\n\nNOTES:\n1. THE CONTRACTORS (GENERAL AND SUBCONTRACTORS) SHALL PROVIDE UNIT COSTS FOR ALL\nSOFTSCAPE AND HARDSCAPE MATERIAL SPECIFIED ON THE DRAWINGS AND SPECIFICATIONS. UNIT\nCOSTS SHALL BE PROVIDED FOR MATERIALS AND INSTALLATION SEPARATELY. UNIT COSTS SHALL\nBE: 'EACH' FOR PLANT MATERIAL, 'SQUARE FOOT' OR 'SQUARE YARD' FOR PAVEMENTS, LINEAR FOOT\nFOR WALL AND FENCE, 'EACH' FOR SITE FURNISHINGS AND SITE AMENITIES, 'CUBIC YARD' OR 'CUBIC\nFOOT' FOR SOIL, MULCH AND OTHER BULK PRODUCTS AND 'EACH' OR 'LUMP SUM' FOR\nMISCELLANEOUS ITEMS. THE UNIT COST SHALL BE FORMATTED TO HAVE COLUMNS FOR; ITEMS, UNIT,\nUNIT COST, TOTAL ITEM COST.\n2. PLANT QUANTITIES ARE PROVIDED FOR OWNER CONVENIENCE ONLY. CONTRACTOR IS\nRESPONSIBLE FOR VERIFYING AND PROVIDING PLANT QUANTITIES SHOWN ON THE LANDSCAPE PLAN.\n3. CONTRACTOR IS RESPONSIBLE FOR LOCATING AND SECURING TREE AND PLANT MATERIAL IN\nADVANCE. BECAUSE OF THE LONG LEAD TIME ON THIS INSTALLATION AND THE RECENT SHORTAGE\nON TREE CROP, CONTRACTOR SHALL RESERVE OR CONTRACT GROW TREES WITH A GROWER.\n\n## Block 6: notes — Notes on special construction techniques.\n\nSPECIAL CONSTRUCTION TECHNIQUES\nECM 3.5.4 (D)\n1. PRIOR TO EXCAVATION WITHIN TREE DRIP LINES OR THE REMOVAL OF\nTREES ADJACENT TO OTHER TREES THAT ARE TO REMAIN, MAKE A\nCLEAN CUT BETWEEN THE DISTURBED AND UNDISTURBED ROOT ZONES\nWITH A ROCK SAW OR SIMILAR EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n2. IN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS THAT CANNOT BE PROTECTED DURING\nCONSTRUCTION WITH FENCING AND WHERE HEAVY VEHICULAR TRAFFIC\nIS ANTICIPATED, COVER THOSE AREAS WITH A MINIMUM OF 12 INCHES OF\nORGANIC MULCH TO MINIMIZE SOIL COMPACTION. IN AREAS WITH HIGH\nSOIL PLASTICITY GEOTEXTILE FABRIC, PER STANDARD SPECIFICATION\n620S, SHOULD BE PLACED UNDER THE MULCH TO PREVENT EXCESSIVE\nMIXING OF THE SOIL AND MULCH. ADDITIONALLY, MATERIAL SUCH AS\nPLYWOOD AND METAL SHEETS, COULD BE REQUIRED BY THE CITY\nARBORIST TO MINIMIZE ROOT IMPACTS FROM HEAVY EQUIPMENT.\n3. ONCE THE PROJECT IS COMPLETED, ALL MATERIALS SHOULD BE\nREMOVED, AND THE MULCH SHOULD BE REDUCED TO A DEPTH OF 3\nINCHES. PERFORM ALL GRADING WITHIN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS BY\nHAND OR WITH SMALL EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n4. WATER ALL TREES MOST HEAVILY IMPACTED BY CONSTRUCTION\nACTIVITIES DEEPLY ONCE A WEEK DURING ACCUMULATION OF THE\nLEAVES.\n5. WHEN INSTALLING CONCRETE ADJACENT TO THE ROOT ZONE OF A TREE,\nUSE A PLASTIC VAPOR BARRIER BEHIND THE CONCRETE TO PROHIBIT\nLEACHING OF LIME INTO THE SOIL.\n\n## Block 8: notes — Notes related to tree preservation calculations.\n\nNOTES:\n1. CHINABERRY AND BRADFORD PEAR ARE INVASIVE TREES AND REQUIRE NO MITIGATION.\n2. ASH TREES REQUIRE NO MITIGATION DUE TO EMERALD ASH BORER.\n\n## Block 11: table — Table listing shade trees.\n\nSHADE TREES\n\n| SYM. | QTY. | COMMON NAME, BOTANICAL NAME | MIN. SPEC. |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [circle with dot] | | EXISTING TREE, TO BE PERSERVED | |\n| [circle with cross] | 2 | LIVE OAK, QUERCUS VIRGINIANA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n| [circle with hash] | 10 | CEDAR ELM, ULMUS CRASSIFOLIA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n\n## Block 12: table — Calculations related to landscaping.\n\nCITY OF AUSTIN\nLANDSCAPE CALCULATIONS:\n\nSTREET YARD AREA:\n| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|---|\n| TOTAL SITE AREA | N/A | 94,203 SF |\n| TOTAL STREET-YARD AREA | N/A | 6,314 SF |\n| STREET-YARD / LANDSCAPE (20%) | 1,263 SF | 5,206 SF |\n\nSTREET YARD TREES:\nEXISTING TREES CREDIT\n| | | |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2\" DIAMETER TO 6\" DIAMETER | 0 | |\n| 6\" DIAMETER OR GREATER | 4 | |\n\nEXISTING TREES CREDIT | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|\n| | 8 CREDITS |\n\nPROPOSED TREES CREDIT | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|\n| | 0 CREDITS |\n\nTOTAL STREET YARD TREES, EXISTING TREES CREDIT PLUS PROPOSED TREES CREDIT:\n| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|---|\n| | 5 | 8 |\n\nLANDSCAPE IN PARKING LOTS:","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":259}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776961761497,"pid":43999,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01DG9AZeC8MvjNuVmkwUXnqq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"From the landscape details and notes, I can see:\n\n1. **Sheet 37** shows tree planting details and tree protection during construction, but does NOT specifically address root barriers for trees near underground electric equipment (EL-2.9).\n\n2. **Sheet 35, Block 6** (Special Construction Techniques) references ECM 3.5.4(D) which covers tree protection and grading requirements, but it doesn't mention root barriers for electric equipment.\n\n3. The landscape notes don't specifically mention root barriers of 4 feet depth positioned 5 feet from underground electric equipment, which is what UCM § 1.10.10.4 requires.\n\nSo for EL-2.9, I would need to check if:\n- Any trees are positioned within 20 feet of underground equipment\n- If so, whether root barriers are specified\n\nFrom the electrical plan vision analysis, I know that underground electric lines run through the site, including a main trunk line running east-west through the center. However, without specific measurements showing trees within 20 feet of these lines and their pull boxes, I may not be able to definitively verify this without additional measurement calls.\n\nNow let me summarize my findings for all 15 checklist items:\n\n**EL-2.1**: Non-utility-compatible trees within 15 feet of overhead distribution\n- APPLICABILITY: YES (OHE present)\n- Without knowing if Live Oak and Cedar Elm are UC or not, and given the measured distances are 43-90 feet from poles, this PASSES for the OHE on the southern boundary\n- However, there's a potential issue with the pad-mounted transformers which are closer\n- STATUS: PASS (based on distances to OHE line/poles)\n\n**EL-2.2**: Non-utility-compatible trees within 50 feet of transmission conductors\n- APPLICABILITY: UNCLEAR (no transmission lines identified)\n- STATUS: N/A\n\n\n- **EL-2.3** through **EL-2.6** all check out as PASS or N/A based on the measured tree-to-pole distances of 43-90 feet, which exceed the required clearances for large trees near distribution conductors, trees near utility poles, and related overhead equipment restrictions. **EL-2.7** involves trees within utility easements, which I need to continue evaluating against the site conditions.\n\n**EL-2.7** reveals critical violations: pad-mounted transformers have trees at dangerously close distances—0.4 feet at Building 8 and 1.9 feet at Buildings 6-7 both fall well below the 10-foot requirement, while Building 1 at 6.2 feet also violates the standard. Only the Buildings 4-5 location at 23.1 feet complies. **EL-2.8** requires verification of underground equipment clearances, and **EL-2.9** involves assessing tree proximity to underground equipment within a 20-foot buffer zone.\n\nFor **EL-2.10**, the landscape plan uses generic symbols rather than identifying actual species near electrical infrastructure—species information exists on a separate sheet but isn't cross-referenced where utilities are shown. **EL-2.11** shows that while some differentiation exists between shade trees and ornamental plantings through symbols, mature heights exceeding 40 feet aren't explicitly designated on the plans.\n\n**EL-2.12** and **EL-2.13** pass since no clearance dimensions appear on the landscape plan at all. **EL-2.14** passes because both tree locations and overhead electrical lines are visible together, enabling visual clearance assessment. **EL-2.15** becomes problematic given the earlier violations—trees may occupy restricted zones without documented Austin Energy approval.\n\nThe pad-mounted equipment violations under EL-2.7 directly trigger EL-2.15 concerns, as these same trees fall within prohibited areas. For EL-2.8 and EL-2.9 regarding underground lines, I cannot verify compliance without explicit distance markings on the landscape plan showing which trees fall within the 5 or 20-foot setback requirements. The electrical plan shows underground routing, but the landscape plan lacks the necessary annotations to confirm whether proposed trees meet these spacing standards.\n\nFor EL-2.10, the core issue is whether tree species are identified on the landscape plan itself for trees positioned near electrical facilities. The plant legend should clearly mark species for any trees in proximity to electric equipment, and I'm not seeing this designation clearly indicated for the trees near the pad-mounts.\n\nEL-2.11 requires that large trees—those exceeding 40 feet at maturity per ECM Appendix F—be visually differentiated on the plans so clearances from overhead lines can be verified. The Live Oak and Cedar Elm specimens would fall into this category, but I need to confirm whether the landscape plan actually distinguishes these larger species from smaller plantings in a way that makes clearance verification possible.\n\nThe plant schedule indicates container sizes (100-gallon versus 45-gallon), which does create a distinction, yet the landscape plan itself doesn't explicitly call out \"large tree\" or reference the 40+ foot mature height threshold. Without that explicit designation on the plans, the differentiation isn't clear enough to satisfy the requirement. I'm marking EL-2.11 as FAIL since the large trees lack proper identification on the landscape plan itself.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3020,"cache_read_input_tokens":75557,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3020},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8dbaef9-50fb-4056-bee6-2c0dccabc43f","uuid":"54042b30-2a5e-4b1a-9287-df80f69a2126"}
